WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.400
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Matt Desmond, who's a professor of sociology

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.000
at Princeton University and also author of
the book Poverty by America.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.610
Really appreciate your time today.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:12.610
Looking forward to this conversation.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:13.920
Speaker 6: Me, too.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:14.920
Thanks for having me.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:20.300
Speaker 1: So when we talk about poverty in
the United States, we know that on average,

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.790
and we can define average a few different
ways.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:26.120
The United States is a very, very rich country.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:30.380
We've all heard, you know, you put Bill Gates
in a room with poor people.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:36.440
The mean net worth gets very high, but it
doesn't really represent the situation for

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.340
everybody other than Bill Gates in that room.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:45.960
Is it a mathematical reality that even though
there are very rich people in the United States

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.390
and that sort of skews these averages, poverty
is still very high.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.660
Do we need to go beyond that to really understand
the source of it?

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.920
Speaker 6: The United States stands alone,
is the richest country in the world with the

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.539
worst poverty.

00:00:59.539 --> 00:01:05.740
So our child poverty rate is double that of
other poor nations like Canada, South Korea,

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:06.740
Germany.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:12.940
Angus Deaton, The Economist, estimated that
over 5 million Americans live in abject poverty.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:17.150
By global standards, getting by on $4 a day
or less.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:21.229
And look, one in three of us live in homes
making $55,000 or less.

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:23.619
A lot of those homes aren't considered officially
poor.

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:28.070
But what else do you call trying to raise
two kids on 55 K in Boston?

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:32.070
So there's a giant amount of insecurity and
poverty in this rich land.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:38.630
Speaker 1: When we think about poverty, part
of what we have to talk about is how we're

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:39.630
measuring it.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:44.369
And as you're talking about, there are people
who, by the definition in the United States,

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:49.820
aren't poor, but that the practical day to
day realities certainly mirror poverty as

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.329
we understand it.

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:55.930
Can you talk a little bit about how the definition
of poverty in the United States has changed

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:56.930
over time?

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:00.500
Speaker 6: So there's a lot of different ways
to measure poverty.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.479
Researchers do not agree on what the best
measure is.

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:08.520
You know, for me, one way to measure hardship
is just to measure hardship.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.640
And so you can look at something like evictions.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.600
They're up 20% since 2000.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.920
Look at the share of families visiting food
pantries, up 19% since 2000.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.420
The number of our homeless school kids is
up 74% since the Great Recession.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:29.590
There's a lot of incredibly troubling signs
that poverty is not only persistent in America,

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:30.939
but growing.

00:02:30.939 --> 00:02:35.750
You know, the poverty line is a is a pretty
flawed measure, but it also tracks pretty

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.830
well with those hardship measures.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:42.390
The government introduced another measure
called the Supplemental Poverty Rate, the

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:47.430
Supplemental Poverty Measure in 2011, and
it counted for a lot of kind of government

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.510
programs the official poverty measure ignores.

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:53.959
But it also counted for like housing costs
and regional variation and cost of living,

00:02:53.959 --> 00:02:56.900
which the official poverty measure also ignores.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.500
And when they released that line, that new
poverty line, the country officially gained

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.460
3 million more poor people, actually, because
any reductions in poverty, offset by counting

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:11.250
aid, were more than kind of canceled out by
counting rising housing and medical costs.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:16.250
Speaker 1: I want to really delve into one
of the aspects you write about in the book

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:20.730
that goes above and beyond defining some of
these terms, establishing what the situation

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:22.770
currently is and what it has been.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:31.300
And you talk about how affluent Americans
knowingly or unknowingly contribute to poor

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:34.060
people remaining poor.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:39.060
And this may be a difficult thing for for
some people to hear and to understand.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.110
Can you talk a little bit about that and how
you lay out the case for the knowing or the

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.040
unknowing aspect of that?

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:51.709
Speaker 6: I ran across this line by the novelist
Tommy Orange, and it goes like this These

00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:56.250
kids are jumping out of the windows, of burning
buildings, falling to their deaths.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.629
And we think that the problem is that they're
jumping.

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:02.530
And when I read that sentence, I was like,
man, that sounds like the American poverty

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:07.300
that they know for for hundreds of years,
really.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:12.310
Our focus has been on the poor and the poor
themselves, the jumpers.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.549
You know, we should have been focusing on
the fire.

00:04:14.549 --> 00:04:15.680
Who lit it?

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.780
Who's warming their hands by it?

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:20.620
And many of us, when we have that kind of
conversation and we want to talk about the

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.440
guy that's just a little richer than us or
a lot richer than us.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.380
But the book is really trying to get a lot
of us.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:32.830
And by us, I mean those of us that have found
some security and privilege in this country

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:38.680
to really yet look in hard in our lives and
realize how we're connected to the problem

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:39.680
and the solution.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.990
A lot of us consume the cheap goods.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:43.780
The working poor produce.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.930
A lot of us are invested in the stock market.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:51.750
Don't we benefit when our savings go up, even
if that savings comes at a human sacrifice?

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:56.139
Many of us benefit from tax breaks and we
don't think of those as welfare programs.

00:04:56.139 --> 00:04:57.139
But they are.

00:04:57.139 --> 00:04:59.479
They cost the government money and they put
money in our pocket.

00:04:59.479 --> 00:05:06.530
And, you know, a lot of us hold on to those
tax breaks which starve anti-poverty spending.

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:11.870
And then we have the audacity to ask how can
we afford to do more as a country?

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:16.520
So I think that when you start looking at
the poverty problem by looking at the fire,

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:20.501
you kind of realize there's so much poverty
in America, not in spite of our wealth, but

00:05:20.501 --> 00:05:22.090
because of it.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:24.340
And I think that opens up new possibilities
for change.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:29.601
Speaker 1: I'm interested in your thoughts
about to what degree there is moral, moral

00:05:29.601 --> 00:05:33.430
responsibility at the individual decision
level.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:38.800
Maybe this is analogous to you know, I've
talked with my audience before about how if

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:46.990
you think taxes should be higher, sending
the IRS more money isn't really policy.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:49.700
It's not a solution to the problem.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:51.130
It's not how we got here.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:59.530
And so you can simultaneously pay, I think,
pay what you legally owe while advocating

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:04.270
that large swaths of people, maybe including
yourself, should be paying more.

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:09.740
If we apply that to the conversation that
we're having about poverty, how should the

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:12.780
individual look at their level of responsibility
here?

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:17.650
Speaker 6: I think we should commit ourselves
becoming poverty abolitionists.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:23.669
You know, we should view poverty not as an
unfortunate aspect of American life, but as

00:06:23.669 --> 00:06:24.669
an abomination.

00:06:24.669 --> 00:06:28.289
And I think like other abolitionist movements,
the movement to abolish slavery, the prison,

00:06:28.289 --> 00:06:33.880
you know, we should recognize that profiting
from someone else's pain corrupts all of us.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:37.650
And so that's a that's a political project
that's going to take renewed political movements

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:38.650
and policies.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.000
But it also is a personal project.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:46.170
And that requires us to shop differently,
invest differently, thinking about taxes,

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:51.160
you know, for those of us that benefit from
this lopsided welfare state, the fact that

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:54.849
the country does so much more to guard fortunes
than to fight poverty.

00:06:54.849 --> 00:06:56.530
Maybe we start a letter writing campaign.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:59.770
We write our congressperson saying like, let's
wind down these things.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:00.800
I don't need this.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:05.960
This is also a neighbor to neighbor conversation
over the fence line conversation, not because,

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:12.080
you know, these little kind of personal moves
will abolish poverty, but those personal moves

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:17.479
multiplied over and over again built a political
will that empowers our lawmakers to act.

00:07:17.479 --> 00:07:21.880
Speaker 1: One of the areas that's a topic
of much, much discussion with lawmakers and

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:27.919
in communities and with neighbors is about
zoning practices, approval boards for condos,

00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:31.199
restricted income condos, all all these different
things.

00:07:31.199 --> 00:07:37.040
Can we talk a little bit about that in the
sense of, you know, we know about the long

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:43.010
history of exclusionary zoning practices,
practices in lending.

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:47.500
Many of us have talked about those realities
we now sometimes see.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:52.080
I mean, recently in Brooklyn, I was talking
with some friends about a project that was

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.890
putting in, I think it was a dozen or so units.

00:07:54.890 --> 00:08:00.180
And the idea is we're going to sell the dozen
units to six buyers and these are going to

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.250
be income limited.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:02.900
So everybody buys two units.

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:06.159
You live in one, we create lower income landlords.

00:08:06.159 --> 00:08:10.970
It all sounds interesting, but is it really
going to are we really going to deal with

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:14.960
this problem piecemeal in that way?

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:19.240
Or does the solution really have to come from
higher and be broader and bigger?

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:23.750
Speaker 6: Yeah, I think both and everything,
all of the above, you know, and I think that

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:28.960
segregation continues to be a driving force
of poverty in America.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.330
There's probably no more soulless phrase in
the English language than municipal zoning

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:34.810
ordinance, you know?

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:37.890
But like, that's a really good way to look
at the soul of a community.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:43.339
You know, and 75% of our land, you can only
build a single, detached family home.

00:08:43.339 --> 00:08:48.230
You know, we build these walls around our
community and we hoard affluence behind those

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:49.230
walls.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:52.779
And that not only concentrates wealth, but
it concentrates poverty outside of our walls.

00:08:52.779 --> 00:08:54.310
So we have to tear down the walls.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:09:00.610
We have to start reaching for broader inclusive
communities, which really means, you know,

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:04.920
going down to your zoning board meeting at
Tuesday night and standing up and saying,

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:06.220
Look, I refuse to turn.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:07.360
My kids opportunities.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:09.400
My kids get living in this place.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:10.400
We got to build this thing.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:12.450
I refuse to be a segregationist.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:16.230
So again, here's a conversation that sounds
very like policy and abstract.

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:17.790
Yeah, but it's also very personal.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.750
You know, it's about how we vote, how we move.

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:24.899
The conversations we're having with our workers
at the WaterCooler, you know, in studies show

00:09:24.899 --> 00:09:29.250
that, you know, Democrats are more likely
to embrace affordable housing in the abstract,

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:33.120
but are no more likely than conservatives
to vote yes in their own backyards.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:38.270
You know, studies show that conservative renters
are actually more likely to vote yes on an

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:41.050
affordable housing project than liberal homeowners.

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:45.160
So I think our values cannot stop where our
property line begins.

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.850
We really have to live out these values in
our own lives.

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:51.010
Speaker 1: How do those values apply or connect
to?

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:54.290
Just to pick something that's often discussed
to get your take on it.

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:58.510
Something like mobile phones and in particular
in the context of activism.

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:03.790
Most of us have heard that and are aware that
mobile phones are connected to so-called conflict

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:06.450
minerals, typically from other countries.

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:12.120
We're now talking outside the U.S., even though
companies like Apple and others will say,

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:13.430
no, everything's fine.

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:15.450
We're ensuring this, that the other thing
isn't going on.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:20.350
Videos have surfaced where we see that there
are very young looking, young looking people.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:21.350
Hard to say.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:23.990
They aren't kids digging by hand, etc.

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:25.470
Then they're manufactured.

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:30.290
They're sold in places like, you know, a T-Mobile
store, Apple store in the United States.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:32.490
And we can look at the wages that are paid
there.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:36.990
And they're not astronomically high, but they're
sort of like, okay, and a lot of these are

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:40.720
creating local jobs, etc., in the United States.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:46.250
How do we take that panorama total and combine
it with the fact that many people who want

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:52.210
to be activists for exactly the issues you're
talking about use the devices to to that end.

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:53.210
Right.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:54.769
So it also plays a role in their activism.

00:10:54.769 --> 00:11:00.750
How do we unwind something so seemingly, seemingly
complicated when we apply the sort of standard

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:02.010
and critique you're applying?

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:06.610
Speaker 6: Yeah, I would love us to apply
that kind of critique to poverty abolitionism.

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:12.440
You know, many of us know not to eat chocolate
from certain brands because of these things,

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:17.970
or we are aware of the climate consequences
of certain purchases or flights or certain

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:18.970
meals.

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:22.670
But, you know, we know that our our cucumbers,
it's local, organic.

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:24.970
We don't know how much the farm hand picked
it.

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:29.940
We know where to get our coffee if we want
to signal certain political values.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:33.110
But we don't ask how much the workers are
getting paid in there.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:37.770
I was in London a few weeks ago and I saw
these stickers on stores and they said, you

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:40.269
know, we plight, we pay a living wage.

00:11:40.269 --> 00:11:42.380
And I went into a store and I said, Well,
tell me about the sticker.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:45.870
And they're like, well, you know, the standard
is here, but, you know, our companies decided

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:47.139
to bring it up to here.

00:11:47.139 --> 00:11:53.730
I would love to see that kind of thing happening
in America, that consumer push and process.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:58.660
And so there's groups like Union Plus that
say, hey, you know, you want a candy bar,

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.200
you want a beer, These are these are union
made.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:04.990
These are then there's organizations like
B Corps that says, you know, these companies

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:07.990
have really high standards of environmental
and social consciousness.

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:10.260
They pay their workers well.

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:15.410
And so I think that like shopping and investing
a little bit in solidarity of the poor makes

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:16.540
a lot of sense.

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:19.070
As you as the cell phone example relates.

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:20.070
Right.

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:25.920
A lot of us kind of feel, you know, embroiled
in these kind of morally compromising networks

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:30.230
and we want out of it, you know, and this
is where kind of an argument for poverty.

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:35.310
Abolitionism isn't just an argument for the
end of this depravity and scarcity.

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:39.672
It's about a freer country, a safer country,
a happier country, and a country where you

00:12:39.672 --> 00:12:40.910
don't worry so much about our kids.

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.820
Speaker 1: You know, I was I was in London
a couple of weeks ago as well, and saw the

00:12:43.820 --> 00:12:47.050
exact same stickers and found them interesting.

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:52.670
Should we be economic nationalists in this
conversation in the sense of prioritizing,

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:56.330
For example, like with coffee, there's very
little coffee grown in the U.S., I think maybe

00:12:56.330 --> 00:12:58.050
some in Hawaii, and that's basically it.

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:01.790
So it's coming from other parts of the world
in having this conversation.

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:07.670
Should we be economic nationalists in the
sense of focusing on the metrics that relate

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:14.790
to the workers in the U.S.? Or should we be
considering the farm in Costa Rica or wherever?

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:19.130
And then with coffee, there's a middle person
that's often involved where sometimes a lot

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:21.149
of that money actually gets sucked in.

00:13:21.149 --> 00:13:24.460
To what degree should we focus on within the
U.S. versus outside of it?

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:29.560
Speaker 6: I think there is and there is something
to be said by starting here.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:35.209
And the reason is building a political will
to abolish poverty in America.

00:13:35.209 --> 00:13:36.480
And I mean that for real.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:41.520
You know, I think we can and should end poverty
in this land of abundance.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:46.920
Getting to that goal, I think, motivates further
investments in countries that have far more

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:51.760
poverty in the United States and and could
could could get us there.

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.610
But if we can't do it here right in our own
backyard, I don't think we have a chance of

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:57.250
doing it abroad.

00:13:57.250 --> 00:13:58.839
So I don't think we have to choose.

00:13:58.839 --> 00:14:02.820
And I think that often we have these kind
of scarcity arguments, right, where we're

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:05.279
kind of setting up this false scarcity narrative.

00:14:05.279 --> 00:14:06.839
I don't think we have to.

00:14:06.839 --> 00:14:11.290
But I think just pragmatically, there is something
to be said about, okay, let's tackle poverty

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:12.910
right here, right now.

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:15.120
Then let's broaden broaden the aperture.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:19.610
Speaker 1: Last thing I wanted to ask you
about, particularly in the context of the

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:25.029
COVID stimulus bill, is the earned income
tax credit has been a topic of discussion.

00:14:25.029 --> 00:14:27.250
I think, generally speaking, it's a good program.

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:28.320
I like the higher numbers.

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:32.500
I think it makes sense in a lot of ways, but
it also has limitations which which you've

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:34.300
talked about.

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:39.260
Certainly it doesn't seem to address the root
causes of poverty and the reasons people might

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:45.320
qualify for an earned earned income tax credit,
child tax credit to begin with.

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:49.209
Can you talk a little bit about that treating
symptom versus disease maybe.

00:14:49.209 --> 00:14:50.209
Speaker 6: Right.

00:14:50.209 --> 00:14:55.850
So the earned income tax credit is kind of
a wage subsidy that our poorest paid workers,

00:14:55.850 --> 00:14:57.740
many of them parents, get every year.

00:14:57.740 --> 00:14:59.230
It's a big part of the safety net.

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:01.570
It's about $61 billion a year.

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:03.589
And for a lot of families, man, it's a life
saver.

00:15:03.589 --> 00:15:04.930
You know, it is a life saver.

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:09.550
It lifts millions of families out of poverty,
but it doesn't address that problem, that

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:10.570
low wage problem.

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:12.920
Do I think that yes, it is a good idea?

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:13.920
Do we need it?

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:14.920
Absolutely.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:19.050
Do I think that we need other things that
really cut at the root of poverty?

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:20.050
Yes.

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:25.959
So those would be things like worker power,
expanding representation on corporate boards,

00:15:25.959 --> 00:15:27.230
for example, for workers.

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:28.230
Yes.

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:32.560
Finding out ways of making organizing easy
instead of this incredibly hard, slow process

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:38.440
that it is now things to really kind of build
up wages and fight worker exploitation.

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:43.459
And, you know, as we're doing that, let's
definitely make sure those families have enough

00:15:43.459 --> 00:15:45.709
to get by with things like the EITC.

00:15:45.709 --> 00:15:47.519
So short term solutions and long term solutions.

00:15:47.519 --> 00:15:54.690
Speaker 1: Do you see that Democrats or Republicans
are definitively better on this issue, or

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:59.519
is that a lot of political framing and posturing
when there may not be as much daylight as

00:15:59.519 --> 00:16:00.569
some would believe?

00:16:00.569 --> 00:16:04.259
Speaker 6: No, I think Democrats are definitely
better on this issue.

00:16:04.259 --> 00:16:09.290
They're I think on the ground level, there's
a lot of Americans that want this, right.

00:16:09.290 --> 00:16:12.130
Most Americans want a higher minimum wage.

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:14.850
Most Americans think the rich aren't paying
their fair share taxes.

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:20.250
Yes, most Democrats and most Republican voters
think poverty now is a result of unfair circumstances,

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:21.740
not a moral failing.

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:26.040
So I think there's a lot of us on the ground
level, neighbor to neighbor to neighbor that

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:29.319
actually agree on these basic issues, economic
justice.

00:16:29.319 --> 00:16:33.139
But our electeds are polarized from us, and
that's a problem that we need to fix.

00:16:33.139 --> 00:16:35.949
Speaker 1: The book is Poverty by America.

00:16:35.949 --> 00:16:40.310
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Professor Matt Desmond, sociology department

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:42.019
at Princeton University.

00:16:42.019 --> 00:16:43.300
Really appreciate your time and insights.

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:44.480
Speaker 6: Thank you, David.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:44.959
Appreciate you.

